# NDM Linux Backup -- settings file template (optional)
# Full details: README "External settings file"
#
# Setup (as your own user, no sudo needed):
#   cp -n ndm_linux-backup.conf.example ndm_linux-backup.conf
#   editor ndm_linux-backup.conf
#
# The file must sit next to ndm_linux-backup.sh (its real location) and is
# only read, never executed:
#   - one KEY=value per line; # starts a comment;
#   - quote values with spaces: v_dest="/mnt/my backups";
#   - lists: KEY=( "a" "b" ), also over several lines;
#   - values are literal: no $HOME, no $(commands). Write full paths.
#     Only v_pre_command, v_post_command and v_on_failure_command may contain
#     "$": they are commands, run later by the script (see README
#     "Notifications").
# An unknown setting or an invalid value stops the script (exit code 2) and
# says which line is wrong.
#
# Every setting, with its default, is described in the CONFIGURE section of
# ndm_linux-backup.sh: keep here only the lines you want to change.

# --- Examples (all commented out; uncomment and adjust what you need) ---

# v_enable_mysql=true
# v_enable_postgresql=true

# v_enable_secrets_backup=true

# v_retention_days=30
# v_retention_min_keep=3

# v_enable_rsync=true
# v_dest_rsync="/mnt/device/backup"

# v_enable_gpg_settings=true
# v_gpg_recipient_file="/root/pubkey.asc"

# A list replaces the built-in one entirely:
# a_source_extra=( "/root" "/etc" "/usr/local/bin" "/opt" "/srv" )

# v_datetime_format='%Y%m%d-%H%M%S'
